## D. Du Pont Analysis

Margin is the main driver. A 20% operating profit margin — exceptional for a cyclical manufacturer — contributes more to ROE than any other factor. Leverage (4.50x) amplifies the underlying 14.9% ROA into 59.7% ROE; without it, returns would still be strong but far less pronounced. Asset turnover (0.74x) is constrained by the capital-intensive model but can improve through inventory reduction, which would lift ROE without adding financial risk. The debt burden of 0.896 shows that financing costs absorb about 10% of operating income before reaching net income — a reasonable cost given the leverage benefit.

---

## E. Strategic Recommendations

### 1. Cut Days in Inventory from 140 to 110 days
This would release ~$3.5B in cash through operational improvement alone. Review demand-driven production scheduling, SKU rationalization, and dealer stocking policies.

### 2. Publish EVA and MVA in investor materials
With $7.8B in FY2024 buybacks under scrutiny, a formal EVA bridge at investor day would show that capital allocation is generating returns well above the cost of capital.

### 3. Add a WACC sensitivity table to EVA reporting
Every 100bp shift in WACC moves EVA by ~$440M. Show the board EVA across 6%-10% to stress-test the value-creation conclusion and prepare for rate volatility.

### 4. Set a minimum cash target of $8-9B
The 0.50x quick ratio and $2.9B increase in LT debt leave less liquidity cushion than in prior years. A formal floor and pre-approved commercial paper facility would reduce exposure to a cyclical revenue decline.

